#!/bin/sh
# The container's wrapper, for the same reason as ~/.config/openab/pi-coach:
# pi-acp spawns PI_ACP_PI_COMMAND as a single binary path, not through a shell,
# so there is no way to smuggle --model in alongside it. Model selection has to
# live here.
#
# What the model choice is based on (measured 2026-07-26):
#   gemini-3.6-flash        OK   holds the "no complete solutions" rule; delivers
#                                the refusal line from the rules, then an empty skeleton
#   gemini-3.1-pro-preview  OK   asks which tier of hint you want; better judgement,
#                                but more expensive
#   gemini-3.5-flash-lite   NO   hands over a near-complete implementation --
#                                must never be used for coaching
#   gemini-3.7-flash        ?    UNMEASURED, and it is what this wrapper currently
#                                selects. Run
#                                  learn/10-model-compliance-eval.sh gemini-3.7-flash
#                                and record the verdict on this line before trusting
#                                it to coach anyone. 3.5-flash-lite is the standing
#                                proof that a newer model is not automatically a
#                                compliant one.
#
# 3.7-flash needs pi >= 0.84.2; the 0.79.9 pinned by the base image does not list
# it. Hence the extra upgrade layer in the Dockerfile.
#
# This no longer matches production, which still runs 3.6-flash. The old reason for
# keeping them identical -- that a shared model is what made a sandbox-versus-
# production comparison meaningful -- is therefore spent: any difference in
# behaviour now has two candidate causes, the container and the model.

# `env -u HOME` is not a typo. It is what makes S4 -- no key inside the
# container -- possible at all.
#
# pi's vertex provider (resolve() in pi-ai's providers/google-vertex.js) runs a
# pure existence check before it ever tries to fetch a token:
#     fileExists(GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS ?? "~/.config/gcloud/application_default_credentials.json")
# If that file is missing it concludes "No API key found for google-vertex" and
# never even enters the ADC chain.
#
# The layer that actually exchanges the token (buildGoogleAuthOptions in
# api/google-vertex.js) behaves differently:
#     GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS unset -> undefined -> full ADC chain -> ask
#     the metadata server
#
# The two consume HOME differently, and that asymmetry is the gap:
#   pi                   expands `~` via os.homedir(), which falls back to passwd
#                        when HOME is unset -> /home/node -> finds the marker file
#   google-auth-library  reads process.env['HOME'] directly; unset means it skips
#                        the well-known file and falls through to the metadata server
#
# So: mount a marker file that is *not* a credential at
# ~/.config/gcloud/application_default_credentials.json to satisfy the first, and
# unset HOME so the second ignores it. The key never has to enter this container.
#
# This is fragile. It rests on two implementation details: the passwd fallback in
# os.homedir(), and google-auth-library reading process.env['HOME'] directly.
# Re-verify after upgrading pi or google-auth-library. The symptoms of it breaking
# are "No API key found" or "Could not load the default credentials".

# --- Where session files live: deliberately on tmpfs ---
#
# The problem: AGENTS.md carries a rule saying "do not read the .jsonl files in
# ~/.pi/agent/sessions/ directly -- one read costs eighty thousand input tokens,
# measured at $0.18". But that is only a prompt rule. The agent has both bash and
# a read tool, so it can reach those files, and nothing stops it on the occasions
# the model does not comply.
#
# Genuinely denying access is not achievable here: pi has to write its own
# sessions, and the bash tool is pi's child process -- same uid, same mount
# namespace. The tool layer's granularity is the tool; the granularity we want is
# the path. They do not line up.
#
# So the goal shifts to shrinking the blast radius, the same move as S4: point the
# session directory at tmpfs. It only ever holds sessions from the current
# container lifetime, which takes the worst case for one read from $0.18 down to
# under $0.01.
#
# The costs, known and accepted:
#   1. Session history dies with the container -> pi_cost.py --days N is
#      meaningless inside the sandbox.
#   2. pi-acp's index lives on a persistent volume (~/.pi/pi-acp) and will point
#      at files that no longer exist after a restart. Measured behaviour: pi
#      quietly builds a new one and does not error; only the ability to resume an
#      old conversation is lost. openab's session_ttl_hours = 6 expires them
#      anyway, so this is acceptable.
#
# Set PI_SESSION_DIR to move it. Note that openab runs env_clear on the agent
# subprocess, so that variable has to be listed under [agent] env in config.toml
# to arrive at all.
SESSION_DIR="${PI_SESSION_DIR:-/tmp/sessions}"

# PI_SESSION_DIR is passed down as well, so pi's own children -- scripts/pi_cost.py
# run through the bash tool, for instance -- know where the sessions moved to.
#
# Why this does not need to go in config.toml: openab's env_clear only applies to
# the openab -> pi-acp hop. From pi-coach -> pi -> the bash tool, inheritance
# works normally, so exporting it here is enough. It also keeps "decide the
# location" and "announce the location" in one place, with no second copy to keep
# in sync.
exec env -u HOME PI_SESSION_DIR="$SESSION_DIR" pi \
    --model google-vertex/gemini-3.7-flash \
    --session-dir "$SESSION_DIR" \
    "$@"
